#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tk-prune-measure — the numbers a pruning pass reads, for one markdown file.

The pruning skill decides what to cut; this bin decides nothing. It MEASURES, and
its exit code says so: every measurement exits 0, however far the file sits from
every target. A file is refused only when there is nothing to measure — a path
that does not exist, or a directory.

WHAT IT COUNTS, and where each number comes from:

- lines: every line of the file, frontmatter and code blocks included, because a
  reader scrolling the file scrolls those too. It is `splitlines()`, so a last
  line with no newline after it counts — `wc -l`, which counts newlines, says one
  less on such a file.
- body words: everything else EXCLUDES the frontmatter and the fenced code blocks.
  A word is a whitespace-separated token holding at least one alphanumeric, so an
  em dash alone is not a word and `**bold**` is one.
- sentences, mean and max words per sentence, sentences over 30 words.
- description words: the `description` of the frontmatter, which is the skill's
  context pointer and is measured against a rule of its own. A file with no
  frontmatter has no description, and that is reported as absent rather than 0 —
  the bin measures any markdown, and a runbook legitimately has none.
- inline evidence: ISO dates, the word "measured", and a count followed by
  "times" or "before". Evidence belongs to the repo's history, not to a skill.
- pointers to other files.
- negations, LISTED WITH CONTEXT. A count alone would be a verdict the regex
  cannot support: `never` steering the agent and `never` inside the name of an
  outcome look identical to it, and only the human reading the line can tell.
- defined terms, and the ones a SIBLING markdown file in the same directory
  defines too — a term whose meaning is written in two places is a term the
  reader has to assemble.

HOW A LINE IS READ. `scan()` walks the body once and hands every later count the
same list of `Line` records: one line, ONE role, its prose computed once. Roles
were seven independent regexes consulted at ten call sites before, and the two
readings drifted — a rule that ended a sentence at one site did not strip its
marker at another. One classification is why they cannot drift again.

THE SENTENCE UNIT is the one thing here that is a judgement, and it is made
explicit because every average below rides on it. A sentence ends at `.`, `!` or
`?`, AND at the end of a list item, a table row or a heading — those carry an
instruction each and almost never carry a full stop. Without that second half
`dispatch` measures 51 words per sentence: its whole palette is one table, and
its rows would read as one enormous sentence with no punctuation in it.

FENCED BLOCKS FOLLOW COMMONMARK, and the consequence is written here rather than
discovered by a reader wondering where half a file went. Same-character fences
DO NOT NEST: inside a ```` ```markdown ```` block, a bare ```` ``` ```` closes it,
and the fence the author meant as the close then OPENS a block that runs to the
end of the file. Five of the 590 markdown files installed on this machine are
written that way, and every renderer breaks them identically. Recovering the
author's intent with a fence stack was rejected: it would make this bin the only
reader in the chain that sees a file the way nobody's renderer does. What the bin does instead is SAY SO — an unclosed
block is reported with the line that opened it, so a number that looks small can
be traced to the fence that swallowed the rest.

WHAT IT DOES NOT MEASURE, so that no report borrows authority this bin lacks:

- WHETHER A SENTENCE INSTRUCTS. Every sentence is measured the same way, and the
  targets read as "no sentence in this file is longer than an instruction may
  be". Which sentences instruct is the pruning table's question, not this one's.
- WHETHER A NEGATION OR A TERM SHOULD GO. Both are reported without a target on
  purpose: a target that fails `writing-for-agents` is not a target.
- WHETHER THE FILE IS GOOD. `--targets` marks each number against a default; a
  file that misses every one may still be the right file, and a file that hits
  every one may say nothing.
- AN INDENTED CODE BLOCK. Only FENCED blocks are held out of the counts. A block
  written as four spaces of indent is measured as prose, and its identifiers are
  counted as words. Telling one from a wrapped list item needs the block
  structure around it, which is a markdown parser; the fenced form is what a
  skill is written in, and this is the line where that stops being true.

FOUR LIMITS MEASURED AND LEFT STANDING. A lens campaign found each of these, and
each is here rather than repaired because repairing it means holding the block
structure of the document — a markdown parser, which this bin is not. Every one
is stated with what it costs, so a report built on these numbers can say where
they stop rather than discover it:

- A FENCED BLOCK DOES NOT END A PARAGRAPH. Two paragraphs separated by nothing
  but a code block are read as one, and their sentences join. CommonMark ends
  the paragraph at the fence; this bin drops the block's lines and leaves the
  prose either side adjacent.
- A FENCE INSIDE A BLOCKQUOTE IS NOT A FENCE. The fence rule reads the raw line
  and `classify` reads it with the quote marker off, so `> ```sh` opens nothing
  and the block's contents are counted as prose — six words instead of one on
  the case that found it. It is the one place where two readings of a line
  survive `classify`, and closing it means quoting state the fence rule does not
  carry.
- PROSE CAN STILL PASS FOR FRONTMATTER. A first paragraph shaped `Word: a
  sentence…` with an indented second line satisfies every test
  `looks_like_frontmatter` makes, and is held out of the body. The gate narrowed
  this from "anything between two `---`"; it did not close it.
- SENTENCE SPLITTING IS QUADRATIC IN THE ABBREVIATIONS ON ONE LINE. `split_sentences`
  re-splits the run before each `.` it rejects: 8 000 of `e.g.` on one line takes
  5.6 s. The worst real file on this machine carries ten, so the cost is
  theoretical — but it is the same shape as the two quadratics this bin already
  had, and it is written down rather than left to be rediscovered.

Usage:  tk-prune-measure <file.md> [--targets] [--json]
Exit:   0 measured (always) · 2 no such file, or a directory
"""

import argparse
import bisect
import collections
import json
import os
import re
import sys

# The targets, as DEFAULTS of the bin rather than numbers written into a skill:
# the environment is the source of truth, and a skill that repeats them grows a
# second copy to keep in sync. `<=` is the only comparison here — every rule is
# a ceiling. Calibrated so the `mattpocock-skills` set passes.
TARGETS = {
    "max_sentence_words": 25,      # the house rule for one instruction
    "description_words": 30,       # the house rule for one description
    "mean_sentence_words": 22,
    "sentences_over_30": 4,
    "inline_evidence": 0,
    "terms_defined_in_sibling": 0,
}

LONG_SENTENCE = 30                 # the threshold `sentences_over_30` counts past

# Row order and labels of the report. Derived from here in text and in JSON, so a
# metric added once appears in both.
METRICS = [
    ("lines", "lines"),
    ("body_words", "body words"),
    ("sentences", "sentences"),
    ("mean_sentence_words", "mean words per sentence"),
    ("max_sentence_words", "max words in a sentence"),
    # the key is a fixed name in the JSON contract, so it does not follow the
    # constant; the label does, and a reader compares the two
    ("sentences_over_30", f"sentences over {LONG_SENTENCE} words"),
    ("description_words", "description words"),
    ("inline_evidence", "inline evidence"),
    ("pointers", "pointers to other files"),
    ("negations", "negations"),
    ("defined_terms", "defined terms"),
    ("terms_defined_in_sibling", "terms defined in a sibling too"),
]

# The indent is CAPTURED, not bounded. CommonMark allows a fence three spaces
# from the margin at the top level and further in when a list item carries it,
# and `\s{0,3}` read the second case as neither an opening nor a closing fence:
# the block's own text was counted as prose, and one space of indent moved a
# fixture from 7 body words to 15. The bound that matters is between the pair,
# in `closes_fence`, not against the margin.
FENCE = re.compile(r"^(\s*)(`{3,}|~{3,})")
HEADING = re.compile(r"^\s{0,3}#{1,6}\s")
LIST_ITEM = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:[-*+]|\d+[.)])\s+")
TABLE_ROW = re.compile(r"^\s*\|")
# `[label]: https://…` — a link reference definition. Each is independent of the
# one above it and none carries a full stop, so without a break of their own a
# block of them reads as a single sentence: measured at 536 words on a real
# CHANGELOG. It is the table problem again, in another construct.
LINK_DEF = re.compile(r"^\s{0,3}\[[^\]]+\]:")
# a setext underline, and a thematic break: both end the line above them
RULE = re.compile(r"^\s{0,3}(?:=+|-{3,}|\*{3,}|_{3,})\s*$")
BLOCKQUOTE = re.compile(r"^\s*>\s?")
WORD = re.compile(r"[^\W_]", re.UNICODE)        # "holds an alphanumeric"

# What tells a YAML frontmatter block from a document that merely opens with a
# thematic break. Without the test `---` had two readings that nothing
# reconciled: a file whose first line is a rule lost every line up to the next
# rule, and a `description:` written in the BODY was harvested as the
# frontmatter's own.
#
# The test is EVERY line, not one of them. A `key:` somewhere in the block is
# exactly what a prose paragraph about a description key also has; what prose
# has and a mapping does not is a line that is neither a key, nor a continuation
# indented under one, nor an item of a list under one.
FM_KEY = re.compile(r"""^(?:[A-Za-z_][\w.-]*|"[^"]+"|'[^']+')\s*:""")
FM_ITEM = re.compile(r"^-\s")
FM_COMMENT = re.compile(r"^\s*#")

# A period that ends a sentence, versus one inside `e.g.` or an initial. The
# lookahead is whitespace-or-end; the word before it is checked in code, because
# a lookbehind cannot be variable-width.
SENTENCE_END = re.compile(r"[.!?]+(?=\s|$)")
# `no` is deliberately absent: "No. 5" is rare in a skill and the word "no."
# ending a sentence is not, and swallowing a real sentence end costs more.
#
# A SINGLE LETTER is not here either, and that is the same trade read the other
# way. Exempting `A.` protects an initial in a name; it also merges "Run step A.
# Run step B. Run step C." into one 27-word sentence, and enumerated steps are
# what a skill is made of while `J. R. R.` is not. So an initial ends a sentence
# here, and the cost is a name split in two.
ABBREV = {"e.g", "i.e", "etc", "vs", "cf", "mr", "mrs", "ms", "dr", "prof",
          "fig", "approx", "al", "st"}

NEGATIONS = re.compile(r"(?i)(?<![\w'’])(do not|don['’]t|never|not|no)(?![\w'’])")
ISO_DATE = re.compile(r"\b\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}\b")
MEASURED = re.compile(r"(?i)\bmeasured\b")
COUNT_EVIDENCE = re.compile(r"(?i)\b\d+\s+(?:times|before)\b")
# A web address, which is not a pointer to a file of this repo however it ends.
# `www.` is here beside the schemes because a scheme-less host is written that
# way in prose, and `www.example.com/index.html` was counted as a file.
#
# The scheme is BOUNDED, and that bound is load-bearing rather than tidy: with
# `*` the class holds `.`, the literal after it is `://`, and a line of dotted
# text makes the engine consume the run and unwind it from every position —
# 6.2 s for 20 000 dots, the same shape of defect `PATHISH` carries a note
# about. No scheme worth resolving is 32 characters long.
WEB_ADDRESS = re.compile(r"(?i)\b(?:[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]{0,31}://|www\.)\S+")
# A path, not a word with a slash in it. The regex matches a path-SHAPED token
# and `scan_pointers()` below decides whether it is one — the split is
# deliberate. An earlier version asked for the extension inside the pattern
# (`[\w][\w./@-]*\.(?:md|py|…)`), where `.` sits both in the star and in the
# literal before the extension. On a line of dotted text ending in no extension
# the engine then retries from every position: measured quadratic, 3.6 s for
# 16 000 dots, and a large enough line never returns at all. Matching the token
# whole and asking Python about its tail is one pass.
# The lookbehind is what refuses the tail of a word: in `KEEP/MOVE` the inner
# `/MOVE` follows a word character and never starts a match of its own.
PATHISH = re.compile(r"(?<![\w/])(?:~|\.{1,2})?/[\w./~@-]*[\w/]"
                     r"|(?<![\w/])[\w][\w./@-]*[\w]")
POINTER_PREFIXES = ("./", "../", "~/")
POINTER_SUFFIXES = ("md", "py", "sh", "json", "html", "txt", "yml", "yaml", "toml")
# Nobody points at a file with a 200-character name. Past this length the token
# is a run of punctuation the shape happened to admit, and reporting it as a
# pointer puts a screenful of noise where a path should be.
POINTER_MAX = 200
# `A **term** is`, with the article optional so the house form `**Pruning** is`
# is found too, and `**term**:` opening a line. The `^` is what anchors the
# second one, not the call site: `.match` would anchor it too, and then neither
# would be load-bearing and no mutation could tell either of them was gone.
TERM_IS = re.compile(r"(?:\b(?:an?|the)\s+)?\*\*([^*\n]+)\*\*\s+(?:is|means)\b",
                     re.IGNORECASE)
TERM_COLON = re.compile(r"^\*\*([^*\n]+)\*\*\s*:")

# One body line, read once: the number a reader scrolls to, the ONE role it
# plays, and its prose with that role's markers taken off.
Line = collections.namedtuple("Line", "number role text")

# The roles whose END ends a sentence, full stop or not. Each carries one item
# and almost never carries a full stop. What is NOT here is the general rule "a
# line with no punctuation ends a sentence": that would cut every wrapped
# paragraph at its line breaks, which is the opposite error and a commoner one.
HARD_BREAK_ROLES = frozenset({"heading", "list", "table", "linkdef", "rule"})


def looks_like_frontmatter(block):
    """True when this block reads as a YAML mapping rather than as prose.

    One `key:` line at least, and nothing that is not one of the shapes a mapping
    is written in: a key, a continuation indented under one, an item of a list
    under one, a comment, a blank line. A block holding a sentence is a document
    that opened with a thematic break, whatever keys the sentence happens to name.

    The key may be QUOTED. Bare-word keys were all this asked for at first, and
    the changesets format writes `"package-name": patch` — 17 of the 165 files
    on this machine that open with `---` were read as prose because of it, the
    delimiters counted as body words along with the rest.
    """
    keyed = False
    for line in block:
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        if FM_COMMENT.match(line):
            continue
        if FM_KEY.match(line):
            keyed = True
        elif not (line[:1].isspace() or FM_ITEM.match(line)):
            return False
    return keyed


def strip_frontmatter(lines):
    """(body lines paired with their 1-based numbers, the frontmatter lines).

    Frontmatter is the block between the `---` on line 1 and the next `---`, AND
    it has to hold a `key:` line. An opening fence with no closing one is not
    frontmatter either: the whole file would vanish from the body, and a file
    that merely starts with a horizontal rule would measure as empty.
    """
    if not lines or lines[0].rstrip() != "---":
        return list(enumerate(lines, 1)), []
    for i in range(1, len(lines)):
        if lines[i].rstrip() in ("---", "..."):
            block = lines[1:i]
            if looks_like_frontmatter(block):
                return list(enumerate(lines[i + 1:], i + 2)), block
            break
    return list(enumerate(lines, 1)), []


def frontmatter_description(fm_lines):
    """The `description` value, or None when the frontmatter carries none.

    Enough YAML for a skill's frontmatter and no more: the value on the key's own
    line, plus the indented continuation lines under it — which covers a wrapped
    quoted string and a `>-` block alike.

    Quotes and block markers are LEFT WHERE THEY ARE rather than stripped. It
    reads like an omission and it is the opposite: neither holds an alphanumeric,
    so neither is a word, and stripping them cannot change a number this bin
    prints. Code that no measurement can tell apart from its absence is code with
    nothing holding it right.
    """
    found = None
    for i, line in enumerate(fm_lines):
        m = re.match(r"^description\s*:\s*(.*)$", line)
        if not m:
            continue
        parts = [m.group(1).strip()]
        for cont in fm_lines[i + 1:]:
            if not cont.strip():
                continue
            if not cont[:1].isspace():
                break
            parts.append(cont.strip())
        # `or None`: a `description:` with nothing after it is not a
        # description of zero words, it is an absent one. Counted as 0 it scored
        # the best possible mark against a ceiling of 30 — a missing context
        # pointer reported as the tidiest one in the set.
        #
        # The LAST one wins rather than the first. A repeated key is malformed
        # YAML either way, and reporting the value a loader would shadow is
        # reporting a number about a line nobody reads.
        found = " ".join(p for p in parts if p).strip() or None
    return found


def opens_fence(line):
    """(marker, indent) of the fence this line opens, or None.

    CommonMark: the info string after a BACKTICK fence may not contain a
    backtick. That clause is the whole reason this is a function — without it a
    line of inline code such as ```` ```echo hi``` ```` opens a block nothing
    ever closes, and every count silently stops at that line.
    """
    m = FENCE.match(line)
    if not m:
        return None
    if m.group(2)[0] == "`" and "`" in line[m.end():]:
        return None
    return m.group(2), len(m.group(1).expandtabs())


def closes_fence(line, fence, indent):
    """True when this line closes a block opened with `fence` at `indent`.

    Same character, at least as long, NOTHING after it — a closing fence carries
    no info string, so a longer opener of the same character cannot be mistaken
    for the close of the block it sits in — and no more than three spaces further
    in than the fence that opened the block, which is CommonMark's allowance read
    where it belongs: between the pair, so a fence inside a list item closes.
    """
    m = FENCE.match(line)
    return bool(m and m.group(2)[0] == fence[0] and len(m.group(2)) >= len(fence)
                and not line[m.end():].strip()
                and len(m.group(1).expandtabs()) <= indent + 3)


def classify(raw):
    """(role, prose) for one body line: exactly ONE role, the markers taken off.

    The role is the FIRST construct the line matches, and the prose is what is
    left after every marker the line carries is removed — a table row inside a
    list item gives up both. Role and prose are two answers about one line, and
    computing them side by side here is what keeps them one reading.
    """
    stripped = BLOCKQUOTE.sub("", raw)
    role = None
    for name, pattern in (("rule", RULE), ("heading", HEADING),
                          ("list", LIST_ITEM), ("table", TABLE_ROW),
                          ("linkdef", LINK_DEF)):
        if pattern.match(stripped):
            role = name
            break
    text = HEADING.sub("", stripped)
    text = LIST_ITEM.sub("", text)
    if TABLE_ROW.match(text):
        text = " ".join(cell.strip() for cell in text.strip().strip("|").split("|"))
    text = text.strip()
    if role is None:
        role = "prose" if text else "blank"
    return role, text


def scan(body):
    """(the body's `Line` records, the line an unclosed fence opened).

    ONE pass. Fenced blocks are dropped here, so no later count carries a rule
    of its own about them, and every surviving line is classified once.

    Fences are matched by their own rule rather than by a fixed length: a block
    opened with four backticks closes on four, and a three-backtick line INSIDE
    it is content. Same-character fences do not nest — the docstring at the top
    says what that costs and why the cost is taken. A block left unclosed runs to
    the end of the file, which is what a renderer does with it too; the line it
    opened on is returned so the report can say the file was cut short.
    """
    out, fence, indent, opened_at = [], None, 0, None
    for number, raw in body:
        if fence is None:
            found = opens_fence(raw)
            if found is None:
                role, text = classify(raw)
                out.append(Line(number, role, text))
            else:
                fence, indent = found
                opened_at = number
            continue
        if closes_fence(raw, fence, indent):
            fence, opened_at = None, None
    return out, opened_at


def words(text):
    return [t for t in text.split() if WORD.search(t)]


def split_sentences(text):
    """`text` cut at its sentence ends — (start offset, sentence) pairs."""
    out, start = [], 0
    for m in SENTENCE_END.finditer(text):
        before = text[start:m.start()]
        last = re.split(r"[\s(\[\"'“‘]", before)[-1].lower().rstrip(".")
        if last in ABBREV:
            continue
        piece = text[start:m.end()]
        if words(piece):
            out.append((first_word_at(text, start), piece))
        start = m.end()
    tail = text[start:]
    if words(tail):
        out.append((first_word_at(text, start), tail))
    return out


def first_word_at(text, start):
    """`start`, moved past the whitespace in front of the sentence.

    The offset a split leaves behind points at the SEPARATOR after the full
    stop, and that separator is the space the previous line contributed when the
    chunk was joined. Attributed as it stands, every sentence that opens a line
    after a full stop is reported on the line above — precisely the sentence a
    reader is being sent to look at.
    """
    while start < len(text) and text[start].isspace():
        start += 1
    return start


def sentences(body):
    """Every sentence of the body, as (line number, word count) pairs.

    A CHUNK is a run of plain lines, or one hard-break line on its own; it is cut
    internally at full stops and forcibly at its end. Offsets are carried so each
    sentence keeps the line it starts on rather than the line the chunk does.
    """
    found = []

    def flush(chunk):
        if not chunk:
            return
        text, offsets, starts = "", [], []
        for line in chunk:
            offsets.append(len(text))
            starts.append(line.number)
            text += line.text + " "
        for offset, sentence in split_sentences(text.rstrip()):
            i = bisect.bisect_right(offsets, offset) - 1
            found.append((starts[max(i, 0)], len(words(sentence))))
        chunk.clear()

    chunk = []
    for line in body:
        # the ROLE, not the emptiness of the text: `classify` decided this once
        # and a second reading of the same fact here is the drift the redesign
        # exists to stop
        if line.role == "blank":
            flush(chunk)
            continue
        if line.role in HARD_BREAK_ROLES:
            # BEFORE as well as after. Flushing only after it left the first
            # bullet of a list glued to the paragraph above whenever no blank
            # line separated them — which CommonMark does not require — and the
            # same document then measured two ways depending on invisible
            # whitespace, with `--targets` flipping ok to over on it.
            flush(chunk)
            chunk.append(line)
            flush(chunk)
        else:
            chunk.append(line)
    flush(chunk)
    return found


def defined_terms(body):
    """(line number, term) for every term this body defines."""
    found = []
    for line in body:
        m = TERM_COLON.search(line.text)
        if m:
            found.append((line.number, m.group(1).strip()))
            continue
        for m in TERM_IS.finditer(line.text):
            found.append((line.number, m.group(1).strip()))
    return found


def sibling_terms(path):
    """{normalised term: [sibling file names]} for the other markdown beside it."""
    directory = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))
    here = os.path.basename(os.path.abspath(path))
    root = os.path.realpath(directory)
    out = {}
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(directory))
    except OSError:
        return out
    for name in names:
        if name == here or not name.lower().endswith(".md"):
            continue
        full = os.path.join(directory, name)
        if not os.path.isfile(full):
            continue
        # a symlink named `x.md` is followed wherever it points, so the name
        # alone does not make a file a sibling: this bin reads the directory it
        # was pointed at and nothing else
        if os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(full)) != root:
            continue
        try:
            with open(full, "rb") as f:
                lines = f.read().decode("utf-8-sig", errors="replace").splitlines()
        except OSError:
            continue
        body, _ = strip_frontmatter(lines)
        scanned, _ = scan(body)
        for _, term in defined_terms(scanned):
            out.setdefault(term.casefold(), []).append(name)
    return out


def collapse(text):
    """(the text with its whitespace runs collapsed, offset → collapsed offset).

    The map is what lets a window be cut around a match without searching for it
    again. Searching was the earlier form and it was wrong twice over: it
    collapsed the whole line ONCE PER MATCH — 12 000 negations on one line took
    10 s — and it found the FIRST occurrence of the token, so every later `not`
    on the line was windowed around the first one.
    """
    out, mapping, i, n = [], [0] * (len(text) + 1), 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        if text[i].isspace():
            j = i
            while j < n and text[j].isspace():
                mapping[j] = len(out)
                j += 1
            if out and j < n:
                out.append(" ")
            i = j
        else:
            mapping[i] = len(out)
            out.append(text[i])
            i += 1
    mapping[n] = len(out)
    return "".join(out), mapping


def line_matches(body, pattern):
    """(line, its text, every match) for each body line the pattern hits.

    Grouped BY LINE rather than yielded one match at a time, so a caller that
    needs the line prepared — collapsed, mapped — prepares it once for all of
    them. Web addresses are removed first: a URL is not a pointer to a file of
    this repo however it ends, and the words inside one are not prose.
    """
    for line in body:
        text = WEB_ADDRESS.sub(" ", line.text)
        found = list(pattern.finditer(text))
        if found:
            yield line, text, found


def scan_kind(body, pattern, kind):
    """Matches labelled with the rule that found them.

    Which rule matched is how a piece of evidence is judged: a date and a count
    are both evidence, and what to do about each is a different question.
    """
    return [{"line": line.number, "kind": kind, "match": m.group(0)}
            for line, _, found in line_matches(body, pattern) for m in found]


def scan_pointers(body):
    """Path-shaped tokens that are pointers: a path prefix, or a known suffix.

    The regex finds the token and this decides — see `PATHISH` for why the
    decision cannot go back into the pattern.
    """
    out = []
    for line, _, found in line_matches(body, PATHISH):
        for m in found:
            token = m.group(0)
            if len(token) > POINTER_MAX or web_host(token):
                continue
            if (token.startswith(POINTER_PREFIXES) or rooted_path(token)
                    or ("." in token
                        and token.rsplit(".", 1)[-1].lower() in POINTER_SUFFIXES)):
                out.append({"line": line.number, "kind": "pointer", "match": token})
    return out


def rooted_path(token):
    """True when a token opening with `/` is a path rather than a slash command.

    `/etc/hosts` is a path and `/tk` is how this repo's own README writes a
    command; both open with `/` and only the second slash tells them apart. The
    bare prefix admitted 21 of the 53 "pointers" that README reported —
    `/tk` eleven times, `/clear`, `/compact` — and `</div>` with them, because
    a closing HTML tag is a slash and a word too.

    The cost is `/usr` written on its own, which stops being a pointer. A
    one-segment path is rarer in a skill than a command is.
    """
    return token.startswith("/") and token.count("/") > 1


def web_host(token):
    """True when the token is an address on the web rather than a file here.

    `WEB_ADDRESS` takes the ones that say so — a scheme, or `www.`. What is left
    is `github.com/owner/repo/README.md`, which ends in a known extension and is
    not a file of this tree. What tells it from `docs/prune/baseline.py` is a
    DOT in the segment before the first slash: a directory of this repo does not
    carry one, and a hostname does. A leading dot is excepted, so `.changeset/x.md`
    stays the path it is.
    """
    head = token.split("/", 1)[0]
    return "/" in token and not head.startswith(".") and "." in head


def scan_context(body, pattern):
    """Matches carrying the line they sit on.

    A count alone would be a verdict the regex cannot support: `never` steering
    the agent and `never` naming an outcome look identical to it, and only the
    person reading the line can tell them apart.
    """
    out = []
    for line, text, found in line_matches(body, pattern):
        collapsed, mapping = collapse(text)
        for m in found:
            out.append({"line": line.number, "match": m.group(0),
                        "context": context(collapsed, mapping, m.start())})
    return out


def context(collapsed, mapping, at):
    """A window of the collapsed line around the offset the match starts at.

    The offset is TRANSLATED rather than reused: collapsing runs of whitespace
    moves everything after them left, and a window cut with the original offset
    drifts right — far enough, on a loosely spaced line, to leave the match
    outside its own context.
    """
    if len(collapsed) <= 100:
        return collapsed
    start = max(0, mapping[at] - 40)
    end = min(len(collapsed), start + 100)
    return ("…" if start else "") + collapsed[start:end] + ("…" if end < len(collapsed) else "")


def measure(path):
    """Every number and every list this bin reports for one file."""
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        raw = f.read()
    # A byte that is not UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD instead of a refusal. `café` saved
    # from a Windows editor is measurable markdown, and refusing it broke the
    # contract this bin leads with; the replacement character holds no
    # alphanumeric, so it never becomes a word.
    lines = raw.decode("utf-8-sig", errors="replace").splitlines()
    body_all, fm_lines = strip_frontmatter(lines)
    body, unclosed_fence = scan(body_all)

    description = frontmatter_description(fm_lines)
    sents = sentences(body)
    counts = [n for _, n in sents]
    body_words = sum(counts)

    evidence = (scan_kind(body, ISO_DATE, "iso date")
                + scan_kind(body, MEASURED, "measured")
                + scan_kind(body, COUNT_EVIDENCE, "count"))
    evidence.sort(key=lambda e: (e["line"], e["match"]))
    pointers = scan_pointers(body)
    negations = scan_context(body, NEGATIONS)
    terms = defined_terms(body)

    siblings = sibling_terms(path)
    shared = [{"line": number, "term": term, "also_in": siblings[term.casefold()]}
              for number, term in terms if term.casefold() in siblings]

    metrics = {
        "lines": len(lines),
        "body_words": body_words,
        "sentences": len(sents),
        "mean_sentence_words": round(body_words / len(sents), 1) if sents else 0.0,
        "max_sentence_words": max(counts) if counts else 0,
        "sentences_over_30": sum(1 for n in counts if n > LONG_SENTENCE),
        "description_words": None if description is None else len(words(description)),
        "inline_evidence": len(evidence),
        "pointers": len(pointers),
        "negations": len(negations),
        "defined_terms": len(terms),
        "terms_defined_in_sibling": len(shared),
    }
    return {
        "path": path,
        "metrics": metrics,
        "unclosed_fence": unclosed_fence,
        "long_sentences": [{"line": number, "words": n}
                           for number, n in sents if n > LONG_SENTENCE],
        "inline_evidence": evidence,
        "pointers": pointers,
        "negations": negations,
        "defined_terms": [{"line": number, "term": term} for number, term in terms],
        "terms_defined_in_sibling": shared,
    }


def marks(metrics):
    """One row per metric that has a target: its value, the ceiling, the verdict."""
    out = []
    for key, _ in METRICS:
        if key not in TARGETS:
            continue
        value = metrics[key]
        out.append({"metric": key, "value": value, "target": TARGETS[key],
                    "status": "n/a" if value is None else
                              ("ok" if value <= TARGETS[key] else "over")})
    return out


def render(report, show_targets):
    """The text report — the same numbers `--json` carries, laid out for reading."""
    metrics = report["metrics"]
    lines = [f"tk-prune-measure  {report['path']}", ""]
    marked = {m["metric"]: m for m in marks(metrics)} if show_targets else {}

    label_width = max(len(label) for _, label in METRICS)
    header = f"{'metric':<{label_width}}  {'value':>7}"
    if show_targets:
        header += f"  {'target':>6}  status"
    lines += [header, "-" * len(header)]
    for key, label in METRICS:
        value = metrics[key]
        shown = "—" if value is None else f"{value}"
        row = f"{label:<{label_width}}  {shown:>7}"
        if show_targets:
            mark = marked.get(key)
            row += (f"  {mark['target']:>6}  {mark['status']}" if mark
                    else f"  {'—':>6}  —")
        lines.append(row)

    # Said where the numbers are, because it is the numbers it explains: a body
    # that stops at a fence is a small file for a reason no metric can show.
    if report["unclosed_fence"] is not None:
        lines += ["", f"note: the fenced block opened on line "
                      f"{report['unclosed_fence']} is never closed — "
                      f"every line after it is counted as code"]

    lines += section("long sentences", report["long_sentences"],
                     lambda e: f"{e['words']} words")
    lines += section("inline evidence", report["inline_evidence"],
                     lambda e: f"{e['kind']:<9} {e['match']}")
    lines += section("pointers to other files", report["pointers"],
                     lambda e: e["match"])
    lines += section("negations", report["negations"],
                     lambda e: f"{e['match']:<7} {e['context']}")
    lines += section("defined terms", report["defined_terms"],
                     lambda e: e["term"])
    lines += section("terms defined in a sibling too", report["terms_defined_in_sibling"],
                     lambda e: f"{e['term']} — also in {', '.join(e['also_in'])}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def section(title, entries, line_of):
    if not entries:
        return []
    out = ["", f"{title} ({len(entries)})"]
    for entry in entries:
        out.append(f"  {entry['line']:>4}  {line_of(entry)}")
    return out


def main(argv=None):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="tk-prune-measure",
        description="Measures one markdown file for a pruning pass: length, sentence "
                    "shape, inline evidence, pointers, negations and defined terms.",
        epilog="Exits 0 for every measurement — it measures, it does not judge. "
               "A path that does not exist, or a directory, exits 2.")
    p.add_argument("file", metavar="FILE.md",
                   help="the markdown file to measure (one per call)")
    p.add_argument("--targets", action="store_true",
                   help="mark each metric against the bin's default target")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", dest="as_json",
                   help="the same numbers as JSON")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)

    if os.path.isdir(args.file):
        print(f"{args.file}: is a directory — measure one markdown file", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not os.path.isfile(args.file):
        print(f"{args.file}: no such file", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        report = measure(args.file)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"{args.file}: unreadable — {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if args.as_json:
        if args.targets:
            report["targets"] = marks(report["metrics"])
        print(json.dumps(report, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
    else:
        print(render(report, args.targets))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
